# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the test suites of a sanitizer build
# (meson.build: test setup "sanitize", which `meson test` uses by itself in
# a build made with -Db_sanitize=address...; `--setup=asan_leaks` passes the
# same file). By hand:
#   LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=tests/lsan.supp build/<name>-test
#
# Only memory of libraries that LeakSanitizer cannot follow is listed.
# Nothing allocated by this tree's own code is.

# fontconfig: a pattern keeps its element array, and a value list its next
# entry, as an OFFSET from the object, not as a pointer (the same structures
# live in its mmap()ed cache files). LeakSanitizer finds no pointer to them
# and reports the elements of every pattern that is alive at exit - those
# of the configuration FcInit () loads on Pango's start-up thread, those in
# the caches of Pango's font map - as lost, with all that hangs off them.
# (What the text code allocates through fontconfig itself is given back in
# walpe_text_release (); a text-part check holds that against the heap.)
leak:libfontconfig.so

# WebKitGTK inside the daemon (web-wayland, web-x11: a sanitizer build of
# walpe-daemon that ran a page and ended through `walpe-ctl quit`): WebKit
# makes one JavaScriptCore context of its own in the daemon's process
# (JSGlobalContextCreateInGroup, called from its main-loop work) and keeps
# it for the life of the process. The eleven literal names its set-up puts
# into JavaScriptCore's string table (24 bytes each, 264 in all) are
# reported at exit, with the X11 page host and with the private one alike.
# Every frame between malloc and the main loop is in libjavascriptcoregtk
# or libwebkitgtk. The line names the function and not the library:
# WebKit's run loop lives in that library too, so its name is in the stack
# of every callback into the web module, and what this tree's code
# allocates there has to stay visible.
leak:JSC::JSGlobalObject::init
